% ============================================================================
% greek-alphabet-standardization — the Greek alphabet variant this stdlib's
% letter-position table uses → when it became the standard 24-letter
% ordering, decoded from a span already sitting unused inside the SAME
% source `greek-alphabet.adj` already cites (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `greek-alphabet.adj`
% (`greek_letter_position(letter, position)`, the 24 letter→position
% mappings): a new `greek_alphabet_standardization(alphabet_name,
% standardized_by_period)` table names WHEN the Euclidean alphabet the
% letter-position table is built from became the standard, decoded from a
% clause already sitting unused inside that table's own `source` field --
% no new WebFetch. That table's schema has room for only a letter and its
% position; the already-quoted sentence also names the historical period
% by which the alphabet had become standard, which never made it into a
% row:
%
%   euclidean_alphabet → fourth_century_bc
%     "by the end of the 4th century BC, the Ionic-based Euclidean
%      alphabet, with 24 letters, ordered from alpha to omega, had become
%      standard"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? greek_alphabet_standardization(euclidean_alphabet, $Period)  % fourth_century_bc
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `greek_alphabet_standardization(alphabet_name, standardized_by_period)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query -- the engine returns the period WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on any alphabet name that is not the one
% tabled here (it never invents a period). The query also runs BACKWARD --
% bind a period and recall which alphabet variant became standard then:
%
%     ? greek_alphabet_standardization($AlphabetName, fourth_century_bc)  % euclidean_alphabet
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table: the already-quoted sentence names
% exactly one alphabet variant and its one standardization period. A
% recall on a different, real Greek alphabet variant this quote does NOT
% name -- `attic_alphabet`, the Athenian local variant the Euclidean
% alphabet formally superseded in 403/402 BC (per general historical
% record, but not named in this already-cited span) -- correctly abstains
% rather than inventing a period for it.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME span already quoted
% inside `greek-alphabet.adj`'s own `source` field, from the SAME
% Wikipedia "Greek alphabet" article that table already cites -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of that
% already-verified quote. `trust consensus` -- the same tier the parent
% table already carries (an encyclopedia is a secondary reference, not a
% primary standards body).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table greek_alphabet_standardization {
    columns alphabet_name, standardized_by_period

    row (euclidean_alphabet, fourth_century_bc)

    source "by the end of the 4th century BC, the Ionic-based Euclidean alphabet, with 24 letters, ordered from alpha to omega, had become standard"
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_alphabet"
    trust consensus
}
